__d(function(e,o,r,i,a,n,t){a.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science \u2014 Social and Political Life",textbook:"Social and Political Life \u2014 I (Textbook for Class VI)",chapter_number:29,chapter_title:"Urban Livelihoods",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a 'livelihood'? How is an urban livelihood different from a rural livelihood?",back:"A livelihood is the way people earn money or resources to meet their daily needs \u2014 food, clothing, shelter, and education. A rural livelihood is mainly based on farming, fishing, or craft-making in villages. An urban livelihood is earned in cities and towns through a much wider variety of activities \u2014 factory work, trade, services, construction, street vending, and professional work. Cities have both organised (formal) and unorganised (informal) work, while villages rely primarily on agriculture.",memory_hook:"Livelihood = Live + Hood = how you LIVE in your neighbourhood. Rural = farm-based. Urban = diverse \u2014 a hundred types of work in every city block."},{id:"fc02",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the organised sector? Name three examples of organised sector workers.",back:"The organised sector includes jobs where workers have: (1) a written employment contract, (2) a fixed regular salary (usually monthly), (3) paid leave (annual, sick, and casual), (4) Provident Fund (PF) for retirement, (5) health insurance or medical benefits, and (6) job security under labour laws \u2014 they cannot be dismissed without due process. Examples: government school teacher, bank employee, police officer, ISRO scientist, IT company software engineer, nurse in a government hospital.",memory_hook:"Organised = O for 'Official' \u2014 official contract, official salary, official benefits. Think of a government office: everything is fixed, documented, and protected. ORGANISED = CONTRACT + SALARY + LEAVE + PF + SECURITY."},{id:"fc03",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the unorganised sector? Name three examples of unorganised sector workers.",back:"The unorganised sector includes jobs where workers have: (1) no written employment contract, (2) irregular daily or weekly wages (no fixed monthly salary), (3) no paid leave, (4) no Provident Fund or pension, (5) no medical/health benefits, and (6) no job security \u2014 they can be dismissed at any time. About 90% of India's workforce is in the unorganised sector. Examples: street vendors, construction labourers, domestic workers (maids, cooks), rickshaw/auto drivers (if self-employed), rag pickers, and small shop assistants.",memory_hook:"Unorganised = U for 'Uncertain' \u2014 uncertain pay, uncertain work, uncertain future. No contract = no protection. Remember: 90% of India's workers = Unorganised. The massive invisible workforce that keeps cities running."},{id:"fc04",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is self-employment? How is it different from working for an employer?",back:"Self-employment means working for oneself \u2014 running your own business, shop, or service instead of being employed by someone else. A self-employed person has no fixed employer, no guaranteed salary, and earns based on how much business they do. Examples: vegetable vendor, plumber, electrician, cobbler, small shopkeeper, auto-rickshaw driver, private tutor, tailor. Difference from employment: an employee works for an employer and receives a fixed wage/salary; a self-employed person is their own boss \u2014 they take all the risk but also keep all the profits.",memory_hook:"Self-employed = your own BOSS + your own RISK. Good day = good income. Bad day (rain, illness, strike) = zero income. Auto driver, chai walla, and street vendor are all self-employed micro-entrepreneurs \u2014 running tiny businesses daily."},{id:"fc05",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a Provident Fund (PF)? Who benefits from it?",back:"A Provident Fund (PF) is a retirement savings scheme for organised sector workers. Every month, both the employee and the employer contribute a fixed percentage of the employee's salary into a PF account. This money accumulates over the working life and is given to the worker when they retire. The Employees' Provident Fund (EPF) is managed by the EPFO (Employees' Provident Fund Organisation) in India. Only organised sector workers benefit from PF. Unorganised workers have no such retirement safety net \u2014 when they are too old or ill to work, they have no savings.",memory_hook:"PF = Piggy bank for retirement. Both employee AND employer put money in every month. Worker gets the full amount when they retire. Unorganised workers have no PF = no piggy bank = poverty in old age."},{id:"fc06",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is migration? Why do people migrate from villages to cities for work?",back:"Migration is the movement of people from one place to another in search of better livelihood opportunities. Rural-to-urban migration occurs when people leave their villages to work in cities. Reasons: (1) farming income is seasonal and insufficient; (2) cities offer more types of jobs year-round; (3) wages in cities (even for unskilled work) are higher than in villages; (4) cities have better schools and hospitals. Migrant workers typically do construction, domestic work, street vending, and transport \u2014 unorganised, low-paid jobs. They often send money (remittances) back to their families.",memory_hook:"Migration = Moving from where there is LESS work to where there is MORE work. Village = seasonal farming. City = year-round many jobs. The price: family separation, living in crowded rooms, and unorganised work. But the income sent home (remittances) keeps village families fed."},{id:"fc07",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the 'dignity of labour'? Why is it important?",back:"Dignity of Labour is the idea that all kinds of work \u2014 whether cleaning, cooking, construction, farming, or professional office work \u2014 are equally important and deserve equal respect, regardless of how much they are paid or what social status they carry. It is important because: (1) every worker contributes to society; (2) without ragpickers, cities would be buried in garbage; without construction workers, there would be no buildings; without vegetable vendors, city people could not easily get food; (3) respecting all workers reduces social inequality and discrimination. In India, certain types of work have historically been looked down upon \u2014 the concept of dignity of labour challenges these prejudices.",memory_hook:"Dignity of Labour = ALL WORK MATTERS. No job is 'below' anyone. The city needs its sweepers as much as its software engineers. Without one, the other cannot function. Respect the person who builds your house as much as the person who designs it."},{id:"fc08",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the difference between a 'wage' and a 'salary'?",back:"A wage is payment for work calculated per day, per hour, or per unit of work done \u2014 paid daily or weekly. Unorganised sector workers receive wages. Example: a construction labourer earns \u20b9400\u2013\u20b9600 per day \u2014 only on days when they work. A salary is a fixed regular payment made monthly, regardless of how many hours exactly were worked that month \u2014 as long as the employee comes to work. Organised sector workers receive salaries. Example: a government teacher receives \u20b945,000 per month including holidays and leave days. Salary = predictable. Wage = variable and uncertain.",memory_hook:"Wage = daily bread. You work today = you get paid today. No work = no pay. Salary = monthly guarantee. Work this month = paid this month (including holidays). Wage earner worries daily; salary earner worries monthly. Salary = more secure."},{id:"fc09",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Bachchu Manjhi pulls a cycle-rickshaw in the city. He came from a village in Bihar. What type of worker is he and what sector does he belong to?",back:"Bachchu Manjhi is a self-employed worker in the unorganised sector. He is self-employed because he works for himself \u2014 he has no fixed employer. He earns based on how many passengers he takes each day. He is in the unorganised sector because he has no written contract, no fixed salary, no paid leave, no Provident Fund, and no job security. He is also a migrant worker \u2014 he has moved from Bihar to the city for better income. If he is sick or if it rains heavily, he earns nothing that day. He likely lives in a shared, cramped room with other migrants and sends part of his earnings back to his family in Bihar.",memory_hook:"Rickshaw puller = Self-employed + Unorganised + Migrant. No boss, no contract, no leave, no PF. Earns per ride. The face of urban India's invisible workforce."},{id:"fc10",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name four types of work that people do in cities to earn their livelihood.",back:"People in cities earn their livelihoods through: (1) Manufacturing \u2014 working in factories producing garments, electronics, food products; (2) Trade \u2014 buying and selling goods as shopkeepers, wholesale traders, or street vendors; (3) Services \u2014 working as teachers, doctors, bank employees, IT professionals, domestic workers, auto drivers, or delivery persons; (4) Construction \u2014 building houses, apartments, roads, bridges, and offices as labourers, masons, carpenters, or engineers. Each type involves both organised and unorganised workers at different levels.",memory_hook:"Urban work = MTSC: Manufacturing, Trade, Services, Construction. Every city runs on these four pillars. From the factory to the shop, from the hospital to the building site \u2014 MTSC covers all urban livelihoods."},{id:"fc11",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why do unorganised sector workers have fewer rights and protections than organised sector workers?",back:"Unorganised sector workers have fewer protections because: (1) they have no written contract \u2014 without a contract, there is nothing legally enforceable; (2) they often work for small employers or are self-employed \u2014 small employers may not be covered by labour laws; (3) they lack collective bargaining power \u2014 they are scattered and rarely form effective unions; (4) many are migrants who do not know local laws or are afraid to complain; (5) the nature of their work (daily wage, casual) makes it hard to prove employment. India has labour laws that are supposed to protect all workers, but enforcement is weak in the unorganised sector.",memory_hook:"No contract = No proof = No protection. Unorganised workers are legally invisible \u2014 the law exists for them but rarely reaches them. This is one of India's biggest social justice challenges."},{id:"fc12",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A vegetable vendor sells vegetables from a cart near your school every morning. Classify her work. What are her advantages and disadvantages compared to a salaried worker?",back:"The vegetable vendor is self-employed and in the unorganised sector. Advantages: she is her own boss, chooses her own hours, keeps all her profits, and does not answer to any employer. Disadvantages: no fixed income (earnings depend on daily sales), no paid leave (a sick day = zero income), no Provident Fund or pension, no medical insurance, vulnerable to weather (rain reduces customers), and can be removed by police or municipality from her spot without notice. Compared to a salaried worker: she has more freedom but far less security and far fewer social protections.",memory_hook:"Vendor = Freedom + Uncertainty. Salaried worker = Less freedom + More security. Both are valid livelihoods \u2014 but the vendor has no safety net if things go wrong. Freedom has a price: risk."},{id:"fc13",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a 'remittance'? Why is it important for rural families?",back:"A remittance is money sent by a migrant worker living in a city (or abroad) to their family back home in their village or town. It is important for rural families because: (1) it is often the main source of cash income for families that depend on subsistence farming; (2) it pays for children's school fees, medical expenses, and family food; (3) it helps build or repair homes in the village; (4) it enables savings and small investments (like buying a cow or a mobile phone) in the village. India is the world's largest receiver of international remittances \u2014 and internal remittances from urban to rural areas are equally significant.",memory_hook:"Remittance = Remote + Assistance. The city worker sends assistance to the remote village. Every rickshaw ride Bachchu completes = a small step toward his children's school fees in Bihar. Remittances connect urban and rural India."},{id:"fc14",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What percentage of India's workforce is in the unorganised sector? What does this tell us?",back:"About 90% of India's workforce is in the unorganised sector. This means that the vast majority of working Indians \u2014 approximately 400\u2013450 million people \u2014 do not have written contracts, fixed salaries, paid leave, Provident Funds, or job security. This tells us: (1) most Indian workers are economically vulnerable; (2) a large portion of the population has no formal retirement savings; (3) most workers are not fully protected by labour laws; (4) India's economy depends enormously on informal, unprotected, low-paid labour. Improving conditions for unorganised workers is one of India's greatest social and economic challenges.",memory_hook:"90% unorganised = 9 out of every 10 Indian workers are without legal protection. If you stood in a line of 10 Indian workers, 9 would be unorganised and only 1 would be organised. The 9 invisible workers are the backbone of India's economy."},{id:"fc15",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does Bengaluru, Karnataka represent the full spectrum of urban livelihoods in India?",back:"Bengaluru represents every type of urban livelihood: (1) Organised, high-income: IT professionals in Electronic City, MG Road, and Whitefield earn high salaries with full benefits \u2014 India's most visible 'new economy' workers; (2) Organised, moderate-income: BMTC bus drivers, BBMP employees, government hospital staff; (3) Semi-organised: Garment factory workers (mostly women) in Peenya and Bommanahalli \u2014 fixed wages but often without full PF/ESI benefits; (4) Unorganised self-employed: KR Market vegetable vendors, flower sellers at Lalbagh, auto-rickshaw drivers, small shopkeepers in Chickpet; (5) Unorganised daily wage: Construction labourers building the new Metro stations and apartments, domestic workers in apartments across the city.",memory_hook:"Bengaluru = India's urban economy in one city. From ISRO scientists to Chickpet cloth merchants to Majestic auto drivers to construction workers from Bihar \u2014 all urban livelihood types exist here, side by side, every day."},{id:"fc16",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is construction work considered unorganised sector work, even though it is physically demanding and essential?",back:"Construction work is in the unorganised sector because: (1) workers are hired by contractors on a daily/weekly basis with no written contracts; (2) payment is daily \u2014 no work, no pay; (3) there is no paid leave or PF \u2014 when the project is complete, the workers are simply let go; (4) safety equipment is often not provided; (5) workers move from project to project with no permanent employer. Despite being physically demanding and essential for building cities, construction workers are among India's most vulnerable workers. Many are migrants from Bihar, Uttar Pradesh, Rajasthan, and Odisha who live in temporary shelters on construction sites.",memory_hook:"Construction = The hands that build your home have no home security. They build permanent structures but have no permanent employment. Every new Bengaluru apartment and flyover is built by unorganised workers \u2014 think of them next time you see a new building."},{id:"fc17",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is ESI (Employees' State Insurance)? Who does it cover?",back:"Employees' State Insurance (ESI) is a social security and health insurance scheme for organised sector workers in India, managed by the ESIC (Employees' State Insurance Corporation). It covers workers earning up to a certain income limit who work in registered factories and establishments. Benefits: medical treatment for the worker and family, cash benefit during sick leave, maternity benefit for female workers, disablement benefit if injured at work, and dependent benefit for the family if a worker dies due to work injury. Only organised sector workers in covered establishments get ESI \u2014 unorganised workers have no such medical protection.",memory_hook:"ESI = Employee's Safety Insurance. Sick? ESI covers your treatment. Pregnant? ESI gives maternity leave pay. Injured at work? ESI supports you. Retired? PF supports you. Together, ESI + PF = organised worker's safety net. Unorganised workers have neither."},{id:"fc18",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the role of domestic workers in cities? Why are they in the unorganised sector?",back:"Domestic workers (maids, cooks, drivers, security guards hired by households) perform essential services that allow urban middle-class families to function \u2014 cooking, cleaning, childcare, and security. Despite working for the same employer for years, most domestic workers are in the unorganised sector because: (1) they usually have no written contract; (2) they can be dismissed without notice; (3) they have no PF, no ESI, no paid leave in most cases; (4) they are not covered under the Factories Act. India has millions of domestic workers \u2014 a large proportion are women from disadvantaged backgrounds. The government introduced the Unorganised Workers Social Security Act (2008) to improve their situation, but implementation remains challenging.",memory_hook:"Domestic worker = essential but unprotected. Works in the home \u2014 keeps the home running \u2014 but has no home of their own rights. Millions of maids and cooks in Indian cities = unorganised, underpaid, and largely invisible in policy."},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Compare the working conditions of an IT software engineer in Bengaluru and a garment factory worker in Bengaluru's Peenya industrial area.",back:"IT Software Engineer (Organised Sector): Fixed monthly salary (\u20b96\u2013\u20b950 lakh per year), air-conditioned office, 8-9 hour workday with some flexibility, Provident Fund, health insurance covering family, paid annual leave (15\u201330 days), job security with notice period before dismissal, career growth and promotions. Garment Factory Worker (Semi-organised, low-income): Monthly wage of \u20b98,000\u2013\u20b915,000 for mostly 10-12 hour workdays, factory environment (hot, noisy), PF sometimes deducted but ESI access variable, limited paid leave, high pressure to meet production targets, mostly women workers, overtime often mandatory before export deadlines. Both are urban livelihoods \u2014 but the gap in income, working conditions, and security is enormous.",memory_hook:"IT engineer vs Garment worker = same city, same economy, completely different worlds. One earns in a month what the other earns in a year. Yet both are essential \u2014 the IT company may even export software for the same brands whose garments are made in Peenya. Urban inequality in one comparison."},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A student says 'Street vendors are a nuisance in the city and should be removed.' Do you agree or disagree? Justify with reasons.",back:"Disagree. Street vendors are an essential part of urban livelihoods and city life. Reasons: (1) They provide affordable food, vegetables, and goods to millions of city residents \u2014 especially lower-income families who cannot afford supermarkets; (2) They are self-employed workers earning an honest livelihood \u2014 removing them destroys their income; (3) They reduce unemployment by creating their own work without depending on employers; (4) They are protected by law \u2014 the Street Vendors (Protection of Livelihood and Regulation of Street Vending) Act, 2014 gives vendors the right to vend in designated areas; (5) Calling them a 'nuisance' reflects a failure to recognise the dignity of their labour. The solution is organised vending zones, not removal.",memory_hook:"Street vendor = city's most democratic market. No entry fee, no middleman, affordable prices. The 2014 Street Vendors Act = India's recognition that vending is a livelihood right, not a nuisance. Dignity of Labour means their right to work is as valid as anyone else's."}]}},2938,[]);